{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning, Mrs. ___. I’m Dr. ___. You were admitted last night with nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain. Can you tell me more about what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, thank you. I’ve been having trouble swallowing—both liquids and solids. It feels like food gets stuck in my chest. And every time I swallow, it burns and hurts."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:03:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That sounds like odynophagia and dysphagia. You mentioned vomiting too?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:04:15",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I vomit a lot after trying to eat. I haven’t been eating much at all lately because it just makes everything worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:05:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Given your history of tracheal cancer and radiation therapy, this is likely related to radiation damage to your esophagus—what we call radiation esophagitis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:06:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not cancer coming back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re keeping an eye on that. A recent CT showed thickening in your esophagus, which could be inflammation or possibly tumor recurrence. But right now, the signs point more toward severe esophagitis from prior radiation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:09:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I had an EGD a few weeks ago. They saw a stricture and ulcers, but couldn’t dilate it because of too much inflammation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:10:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, I reviewed your records. The biopsy showed radiation changes, no HSV, CMV, or fungal infection. You completed a course of fluconazole, so we won’t repeat that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:12:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But I’m still not getting better. Why?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:13:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The inflammation is still active. We’ve started you on a dissolving lansoprazole since you’ve had trouble swallowing pills. We’re also adding sucralfate and reglan to help coat the esophagus and improve motility."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:14:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Are those going to help with the pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:15:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They’ll help protect the lining and reduce irritation. For pain, we’re continuing your MS Contin and will use IV morphine as needed. We want to keep you comfortable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:16:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I’ve also been coughing more—sometimes with phlegm."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:17:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you had a low-grade fever yesterday and a productive cough. Your chest X-ray was clear, and urine culture was negative. We think it’s viral tracheobronchitis, possibly from radiation effects or a mild infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I don’t want antibiotics unless I really need them."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2174-03-14 08:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re right. No signs of bacterial pneumonia, and your fever resolved on its own. We’ll stick with supportive care—benzonatate for the cough."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We reviewed your CT scan from yesterday. It shows worsening esophageal wall thickening, consistent with progressive esophagitis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is it cancer?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s hard to tell definitively on imaging. The changes could be from inflammation or recurrence. But given your prior biopsies, we’re leaning toward radiation injury for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what’s next?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "GI was consulted. Because of the severe ulceration, they don’t recommend dilation now—it could cause a tear. Instead, they suggest placing a feeding tube to let your esophagus heal."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:07:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A feeding tube? I was hoping to avoid that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand. But with your dysphagia and vomiting, you’re not getting enough nutrition. The tube will help you heal and prevent further weight loss."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:10:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will it be permanent?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, not necessarily. Once the inflammation improves, we can reassess for dilation and possibly remove it. This is a temporary support."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:13:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I trust you. When would it be placed?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2174-03-15 09:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s scheduled for tomorrow morning, guided by X-ray. The procedure is called a PEJ—percutaneous endoscopic jejunostomy. It goes directly into your small intestine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2174-03-16 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—your PEJ tube was placed successfully this morning. It’s in the right spot, and we’ve started tube feeds at a slow rate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2174-03-16 10:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel some pain around the tube site."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2174-03-16 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s expected. There will be discomfort for a few weeks as the tract heals. We’ll keep your pain managed with your regular medications."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2174-03-16 10:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve also been constipated. Haven’t had a bowel movement in days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2174-03-16 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The KUB showed moderate stool buildup. We’ve prescribed senna and docusate. You’ve refused them so far—can we start today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2174-03-16 10:07:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I guess so. I just hate being dependent on laxatives."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2174-03-16 10:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand, but it’s important to prevent pain and bloating. We’ll use the lowest effective dose."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2174-03-17 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your nausea has improved since the tube was placed. You haven’t vomited in over 24 hours."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2174-03-17 08:31:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, that’s a relief. But I still have burning in my chest when I swallow saliva."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2174-03-17 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s from the esophagitis. We’re continuing high-dose pantoprazole and sucralfate. Healing takes time—weeks, not days."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2174-03-17 08:34:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been on prednisone for my arthritis, but I never started the taper after my last hospitalization."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2174-03-17 08:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re starting the taper now—3 tablets a day for 3 days, then gradually down. Stopping steroids suddenly can be dangerous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2174-03-17 08:37:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll follow the plan."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2174-03-18 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re adding nortriptyline at night for nerve pain. It may also help with sleep and chronic pain from fibromyalgia."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2174-03-18 14:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will it make me sleepy?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2174-03-18 14:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It might, especially at first. That’s actually helpful at bedtime. We’ll start low and go slow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2174-03-19 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs show your hemoglobin has been dropping—from 10.2 to 8.1. But no sign of bleeding. Likely due to chronic disease and poor intake."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2174-03-19 09:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Am I going to need a transfusion?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2174-03-19 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not right now. Your levels are stable, and you’re not symptomatic. With tube feeds, your nutrition should improve, and your blood counts may recover."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2174-03-20 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your cough is still there, but your repeat chest X-ray is clear. We’ll keep monitoring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2174-03-20 10:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m still producing phlegm."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2174-03-20 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Given your history, it could be from chronic bronchitis or post-radiation changes. We’ll continue guaifenesin to help loosen secretions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2174-03-21 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The abdominal X-ray shows your feeding tube is in good position and no obstruction. Stool load is improving since you started the laxatives."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2174-03-21 08:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The pain around the tube is a little better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2174-03-21 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s good. We’ve increased your morning MS Contin to 30mg for better pain control. But we can’t go higher—you got confused the last time we tried."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2174-03-21 08:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I don’t want to be out of it. I’d rather have some pain than be confused."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning for discharge tomorrow. You’re stable, your symptoms are controlled, and you’re tolerating tube feeds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What will my care be like at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll go home with visiting nurses. They’ll help with tube care, feeding, and meds. You’ll continue pantoprazole, sucralfate, and a short course of fluconazole."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I thought I already finished fluconazole?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You did, but we’re giving a short 9-day course as a precaution. Also, your prednisone taper continues—follow the new schedule exactly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:07:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the feeding tube?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:09:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll receive tube feeds overnight—120 mL per hour for 14 hours. Flush with water before and after. You can still take meds by mouth or through the tube, except your MS Contin—don’t crush it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:10:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What if I can’t swallow it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Call your doctor. Don’t crush or cut it. We may need to adjust."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:13:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And when do I follow up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2174-03-22 09:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "GI in two weeks. They’ll reassess for possible dilation. Keep taking care of the tube site and report any redness, swelling, or leakage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2174-03-23 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You’re cleared for discharge. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2174-03-23 08:01:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. Still some discomfort, but I understand it will take time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 68,
      "time": "2174-03-23 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. The tube site will hurt for a few more weeks. But you’re on the right path. We’ll support you every step of the way."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 69,
      "time": "2174-03-23 08:04:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you for explaining everything. I feel prepared."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 70,
      "time": "2174-03-23 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re very welcome. Take care, and we’ll see you in follow-up."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2174-03-14 07:18:00",
    "admission_end": "2174-03-23 19:12:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "Mrs. ___ was admitted for intractable nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, dysphagia, and odynophagia, all stemming from severe radiation esophagitis related to prior treatment for tracheal cancer. Despite a recent hospitalization and treatment with high-dose pantoprazole and fluconazole, her symptoms progressed, leading to poor oral intake and dehydration. A CT scan showed worsening esophageal wall thickening, and GI consultation confirmed she was not a candidate for dilation due to severe ulceration. A percutaneous endoscopic jejunostomy (PEJ) tube was placed to enable enteral feeding and allow the esophagus to heal. Her nausea and vomiting resolved post-procedure, and pain was managed with adjusted opioid dosing. A prednisone taper was initiated for rheumatoid arthritis, and nortriptyline was started for neuropathic pain. She was discharged home with nursing services, a detailed medication regimen, and plans for outpatient GI follow-up to reassess for dilation in several weeks.",
    "problems": [
      "Other esophagitis",
      "Chronic systolic heart failure",
      "Congestive heart failure, unspecified",
      "Late effect of radiation",
      "Abdominal pain, other specified site",
      "Nausea with vomiting",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of bronchus and lung",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of breast",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of trachea",
      "Acute bronchitis",
      "Myalgia and myositis, unspecified",
      "Dysphagia, unspecified",
      "Rheumatoid arthritis",
      "Tobacco use disorder",
      "Knee joint replacement"
    ]
  }
}
